Title: Flag 6 rescores 2,688 works on every corpus rebuild: the relevance cache is DOI-keyed
Created: 2026-07-27
Author: HDMX-coding-agent

--- log ---
2026-07-27T13:32Z HDMX-coding-agent created
2026-07-27T13:35Z HDMX-coding-agent note found during 0347's corpus rerun. Measured, not inferred: 10,226 Flag-6 candidates, 7,538 cache hits, 2,688 needing a fresh reranker score, of which 1,908 have no DOI at all. The rerun then proved the rescoring reproduces bit-identically on padme with the cached model — so this is a cost and fragility ticket, not a correctness one.

--- body ---
## Context

`filter_flags_llm._load_llm_cache` keys the relevance-score cache on the
normalised DOI:

    cache[row["doi"]] = float(score_str)

A work without a DOI therefore can never be cached, and is rescored by the
cross-encoder on every `corpus_filter --extend` run. Measured on the current
corpus (43,179 works):

| | count |
|---|---|
| Flag-6 candidates | 10,226 |
| already cached by DOI | 7,538 |
| needing a fresh score | **2,688** |
| of those, no DOI at all (never cacheable) | **1,908** |

The 1,908 are structural: grey literature, the curated UNFCCC/OECD key
documents (0% and 12% DOI carriage), and other no-DOI records. Every corpus
rebuild pays for them again.

## Why it matters

Cost is the smaller half. The real issue is that a stage the project treats as
deterministic depends on a model load:

- **A rebuild needs the GPU, the model, and its full dependency closure.**
  0347's rerun died at `CrossEncoder.__init__` because `protobuf` was absent
  from the venv — an undeclared transitive of the sentencepiece tokenizer
  conversion. Because no-DOI records are never cached, there is no cache-only
  path that could have skipped the model entirely.
- **Reproducibility rests on an unpinned model + library combination.** If
  `sentence-transformers`, `transformers`, or the model revision moves, 2,688
  works can silently change `llm_irrelevant`, which moves `refined_works.csv`
  row counts and every downstream statistic. Nothing pins the model revision
  and nothing detects the drift.

Good news, and the reason this is not filed as a defect: on 0347's rerun the
2,688 rescored works came out **bit-identical** to the frozen 2026-07-24 pass
(`extended_works.csv` differed only in the 245 `keywords_provenance` cells).
So the reranker is reproducible on this machine with the cached model. The
exposure is to future environment drift, not to a present error.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/filter_flags_llm.py` — `_load_llm_cache`, `_save_llm_cache`,
  `_cache_key`, `_identify_candidates`, `_load_reranker_model`
- `data/catalogs/llm_relevance_cache.csv` — 40,219 entries, DOI-keyed
- `config/corpus_filter.yaml` — `llm_relevance` block (model, threshold)
- `scripts/harvest/corpus_filter.py` — the `--extend` entry point

## Actions

1. Give the cache a fallback key for no-DOI records. `source_id` is the
   natural choice — `in_v1` already uses exactly this DOI-then-source_id
   strategy (`corpus_filter.add_in_v1_column`), so the pattern is established
   in the codebase and needs no invention. Keep the `_cache_key` config hash
   so a model or query change still invalidates.
2. Pin the model revision in `config/corpus_filter.yaml` and fold it into the
   cache's config hash, so a model update invalidates the cache loudly instead
   of silently rescoring differently.
3. Once 1 lands, a fully warm cache makes the model load unnecessary — skip
   `_load_reranker_model` when no candidate needs a fresh score, so a rebuild
   on a machine without the GPU or the model closure still succeeds.

## Test

- A no-DOI work scored once is served from cache on the second run: assert
  `_load_reranker_model` is not called at all when every candidate is cached
  (the assertion that would have caught the eager load).
- Cache round-trip for a record with an empty DOI and a populated
  `source_id`.
- A changed model revision in config invalidates the cached entry.

## Verification

- [ ] Candidate count needing a fresh score drops to 0 on a second
      consecutive `--extend` with no corpus change
- [ ] `--extend` completes on a machine with no reranker model available,
      given a warm cache
- [ ] `extended_works.csv` byte-identical across the two runs

## Invariants

- Flag 6's decisions must not change as a side effect of adding the fallback
  key: byte-compare `extended_works.csv` before and after, per the
  refactor-validation rule.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] No-DOI works are cached across runs
- [ ] Model revision pinned and part of the cache invalidation key
- [ ] A warm-cache rebuild needs neither GPU nor model
